question.text:
**Write the opening paragraph (80 to 120 words) of a story about a lost object.**

Your opening should:
- begin in the middle of something (an object, an action or a line of speech), not with waking up;
- name your main character;
- include one precise, specific detail about the setting;
- show a feeling through an action rather than naming it.

**Write a story that begins with the sentence: *"The message had been sent to the wrong person."***

Write around 350 to 500 words. Aim to:

- follow the five-part shape (hook, build, turning point, climax, resolution);
- keep to one main character, one setting and a short stretch of time;
- show at least one feeling through action rather than naming it;
- include a short exchange of correctly punctuated dialogue;
- vary your sentence lengths, using at least one deliberately short sentence;
- end with a resolution that links back to the opening (not a dream).

*(In the exam this task would be worth 40 marks. Here it is checked against eight key features.)*

practiceQuestions.text:
**Write a story about a journey that does not go as planned.**

Write around 300 to 450 words. Aim to:
- open in the middle of the journey with a precise detail;
- keep to one character and a short stretch of time;
- include a clear turning point where the plan fails;
- show a feeling through action;
- use at least one short sentence for impact;
- end with a resolution that links back to the opening.

*(In the exam this task would be worth 40 marks. Here it is checked against six key features.)*
